Java OOP consists of four access scopes for classes, properties, and methods: private, protected, default, and public.
Private scope : allows access only within the class. In example 4, we see that the `name` attribute of the `Person` class has a private scope , which means that attribute of the `Person` class can only be accessed within the `Person` class itself.
Protected scope : This is a type of scope that allows access from inside or outside the package. If it's outside the package, it must be through inheritance. Both private and protected scopes only apply within a class, such as properties, methods, etc. They cannot be applied to the access scope of a class or interface. Here's a small example of protected .
// Định nghĩa lớp X trong gói a
package a;
public class X{
protected int x1, x2;
protected int plus(){
return x1 + x2;
}
}
// Định nghĩa lớp Y trong gói a
public class Y{
public static main(String args[]){
X c = new X();
c.x1 = 1;
c.x2 = 2;
System.out.print(c.plus());
// Ta có thể thấy rằng chúng ta hoàn toàn truy xuất được đến các thuộc tính hay phương thức có trong X
}
}
// Định nghĩa lớp Z trong gói b
package b;
public class Z extends a.X {
Z c = new X();
// Khi này để truy xuất được đến các thuộc tính, phương thức trong X ta cần để Z kế thừa X.
}
}
Default scope : This is the default scope; nothing needs to be specified when declaring it (the height attribute in example 4). For this default scope , access is limited to within the same package.
Public scope : This is the broadest scope, accessible from anywhere in the project. (In example 4, the Person class has a public scope, so the Person class can be called from anywhere in the project).
4. Properties in OOP
OOP generally has four characteristics: abstraction, inheritance, encapsulation, and polymorphism.
Abstraction : Abstract means generalizing something without going into too much detail, yet the listener still understands what it is. In OOP, abstraction means selecting the necessary attributes and methods of an object to solve a problem. In reality, an object may have many attributes and methods, but they may not be relevant or needed in the given problem. For example : in a cat management problem, for instance, we only need to generalize the cat object into a class of type:
public class cat{
private int ID;
private String species; // chủng loại
private String origin; // Xuất xứ
private double weight; // cân nặng
// Các phương thức
public void input(){
}
}
}
We don't need to jump into the details of attributes like: number of fur, number of legs, number of toes on each foot, number of ears, number of eyes, ... or methods like: eating, moving, ... But it's enough that the listener understands it's a cat, and more importantly, the attributes and methods we create can all be used to solve the problem of cat management.
Inheritance : Simply means reusing attributes and methods from another class without having to rebuild them from scratch. Example 6:
